  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A builder has $320$ meters of fencing and she wants to enclose a rectangular parking lot. One side next to a building and so this side doesn't need to be fenced. Find $x+y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are the dimensions of the parking lot that has the greatest area.",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We draw a diagram, and introduce variables for the length and width of the fencing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/275803f7f72469a8427b137b5e8523475cc0605f033bedaa2280a5d28f453c38.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $320\\,\\text{m}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=320.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =320 \\\\ x & =320-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=320-2y$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(320-2y)y \\\\ A & =320y-2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{\u2032}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2032}(y)=320-4y,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(y) & =0 \\\\ 320-4y & =0 \\\\ 4y & =320 \\\\ y & =\\frac{320}{4} \\\\ y & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $y=80$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2033}(y)=-4\\,\u27f9\\,{A}^{\u2033}(80)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{\u2033}(80)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=80$ is indeed a maximum of $A(y).$\n\nFinally, when $y=80$, we have \n\n$$\nx=320-2(80)=160.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the field with the largest area are $x=160$ and $y=80,$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+y=240.\n$$",
